"Surah Tebbet foretells the ruin of Abu Lahab and his wife. The word \"tebbet\" bears the meaning of both the report that 'he perished' and the curse of 'may he be ruined'. Due to his hostility toward our Prophet, Abu Lahab died in despair after the Battle of Badr, and thus the surah's prophecy was fulfilled. His two hands—that is, all his means and efforts—came to naught, and he was ruined.\n\nThe reason Abu Lahab is specifically mentioned in the surah is that, despite being the Prophet's uncle, he persisted in disbelief and therefore deserved the severest condemnation. Furthermore, the name \"Abu Lahab\" literally means 'father of flame'; this indicates that he is destined for Hell and represents all fire-spitting disbelievers. Lineage and honor could not save him, and this served as an admonition to other people.\n\nAbu Lahab's wealth and what he earned (his children, commerce, his hostilities) availed him nothing and did not save him from destruction. The word 'kesb' mentioned in the verse encompasses all these gains. Along with all of these, he will enter a blazing fire—Hell.\n\nAbu Lahab's wife, Umm Jamil, will also enter Hell and be depicted as a 'carrier of firewood'. This expression is an allusion to her carrying thorns in the worldly life to torment the Prophet or to her spreading malicious gossip. Furthermore, it should be remembered that the firewood of Hell is the disbelievers. His wife sharing in the punishment will increase Abu Lahab's suffering.\n\nAround her neck, instead of a necklace, there will be a sturdy rope called \"mesed\". This rope is a strong cord twisted from palm fibers. For a woman who lived in adornment and honor in this world to be humiliated in the Hereafter as a carrier of firewood with a rope around her neck is the bitter consequence of disbelief and ingratitude. The surah concludes with this exemplary scene."